# THE ORDINAL RANK OF EVERY LAYER IN `F`: the order in which a fusion may be evaluated. One row
# per layer, `rank` 0 where nothing lies beneath it.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS WHAT WELL-FOUNDEDNESS ACTUALLY GIVES YOU. A well-founded relation has a rank
#    function, `rank(x) = sup{rank(y)+1 : y R x}`, and the precondition that
#    *no name expands to itself* IS the guarantee that one exists. A cycle is exactly the case
#    where it does not, which is what "no order exists in which to evaluate the stack" means.
#
# ⛔⛔ RANK IS THE EVALUATION ORDER AND `depth` IS NOT. composition/descent.sqlc's `depth` is per
#    PATH; the rank is the MAXIMUM over all paths. A layer reachable at depth 1 and at depth 2
#    must not be evaluated until both arrivals are ready, so a walk that evaluates in depth order
#    can compute a fusion before one of its parts exists. ⭐ On this corpus min and max agree on
#    every row, so the difference is unexercised BY LUCK: a jagged partition is what separates
#    them, and checks/jagged_layer is the same phenomenon in the other algebra.
#
# ⭐ IT IS A WINDOW AND NOT A SECOND WALK. descent.sqlc has already done the recursion, so the
#   rank is one aggregate over its closure. Proposing another recursive CTE for this is proposing
#   to recompute something the data already carries.
-- composition/descent.sqlc aggregated to the deepest arrival under each layer.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       coalesce(max(d.depth), 0) AS rank,
       count(d.filing)           AS arrivals
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
) d ON d.root_filing = l.filing AND d.root_layer = l.layer
GROUP BY l.filing, l.layer
